{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_204","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat agent/report.md agent/suggestions.md'","aggregated_output":"# Predictable clocks, uncertain volatility\n\nThis report is a working draft; the research campaign is still in progress and no champion has been nominated.\n\n## Context\n\nSynth scores simulated price paths by CRPS. We study all three configurations: crypto over 24 hours, commodities/equities over 24 hours, and crypto over one hour. All evaluations use the installed official backtester and 1000 paths. Prices come exclusively from the supplied snapshot, ending November 8, 2026. We optimize CRPS rather than field rank.\n\nThe starter uses seven days of minute returns to set a single Gaussian volatility. Its largest errors have two different explanations: a slow response to a crypto volatility expansion in August, and predictable trading-session variation in commodity/equity perpetuals. Flat candles remain real observations. Missing prices remain missing, and returns are never calculated across a dropped gap.\n\n## Approach\n\nThe current checkpoint estimates a seasonal variance profile, removes it from historical returns, estimates volatility at several exponential time scales, and restores the known future clock profile. Seasonal artifacts are timestamped and trained on at most 120 earlier days. Historical forecasts select only an earlier fit.\n\nA small volatility-only boosted regression predicts future absolute-return scale relative to that seasonal EWMA. Separate 1h and 24h fits use completed forward labels only; training and validation are separated chronologically with a purge at the split. A serving/training parity check gives identical float32 features. No price drift is learned.\n\nThe event calendar, innovation tails, simulation sampling and robust/simple scale alternatives remain under investigation. Their results will be included whether successful or not.\n\n## Experiments\n\nDevelopment scored windows end August 24 and October 12, 2026; confirmation windows end July 20 and November 8. Each covers seven days, sampled every 120 minutes with a 17-minute offset. The one-hour score always uses the latest formula. These grids are for paired comparisons, not claims about absolute live calibration or rank.\n\nFast variance adaptation alone is not a universal improvement: a six-hour half-life worsens 24h crypto CRPS during the August expansion. Absolute-return estimators are more robust. Seasonality provides a much larger improvement for equities, and its benefit holds in every tested commodity/equity asset-window cell.\n\nUTC clock profiles are deceptive in summer-only tests. In the November switch week, UTC raises commodity/equity CRPS by 1.67% against New York time, including a 6.06% AAPLX regression. Gold and oil differ from the equities. A March test spanning the US/UK clock mismatch is in progress.\n\n## Results\n\nProvisional CRPS at 1000 paths, averaged over the paired development windows:\n\n| Model | CRYPTO_24H | COM_EQU_24H | CRYPTO_1H |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Seeded starter | 4802.283427 | 2004.899638 | 1027.941065 |\n| Seasonal EWMA, New York | 4716.953273 | 1799.685377 | 998.559771 |\n| Learned volatility-only scale | 4693.825150 | 1772.589566 | 981.761767 |\n\nOn the paired confirmation windows, starter CRPS is 3831.087636 / 3114.391572 / 814.313609 and learned-scale CRPS is 3687.472104 / 2867.838497 / 770.135527 in the same configuration order. The learned adjustment improves the seasonal EWMA in 71 of 72 asset-window cells across development and confirmation.\n\nGeneration takes hundredths of a second per prompt on this machine, including the evaluator's load/write overhead. Full interface, gap, seed and actual-field-prompt validations are still pending.\n\n## Limitations\n\nThe windows are historical research samples, not an untouched final holdout. The strongest tests still need denser actual prompt times and wider regime coverage. Event types occur only a few times per year, and contemporaneous asset reactions are correlated. Earnings dates have coarse session anchors. Rare unscheduled shocks cannot be predicted by a calendar.\n\n## What we would do with more budget\n\nThis section will be finalized at landing, after the planned distribution, event, robustness and independent-regime experiments.\n# Suggestions outside this model's scope\n\n- Expose interval-level CRPS details in the official backtest result. The validator already computes them; retaining them avoids instrumentation and makes price-versus-volatility diagnosis easier.\n- Preserve the calendar's distinction between precise release timestamps and coarse earnings session anchors in the production interface. A date can be known while its exact release minute remains uncertain.\n- Report valid realized-price coverage and missed-prompt counts alongside mean CRPS. Sparse references and unanswered prompts must remain visible when comparing models.\n- Document the issuance lead and candle close convention next to the serving interface. Here the context ends three minutes before a 24h prompt and two minutes before a 1h prompt.\n\nNo operator miner models were encountered or used. All implementation ideas and fitted artifacts in this campaign use the allowed public scoring code, supplied snapshot/calendar, and this campaign's own research.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_205","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat agent/vol_models.py agent/fit_seasonality.py'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_205","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat agent/vol_models.py agent/fit_seasonality.py'","aggregated_output":"\"\"\"Causal volatility-estimation ablations, Gaussian paths held fixed.\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nimport os\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\nCONTEXT_DAYS = 7\nVARIANT = os.environ.get(\"SYNTH_RESEARCH_VARIANT\", \"ewma_mix\")\n\n\ndef valid_returns(context):\n    p = np.asarray(context[\"close\"], dtype=float)\n    good = np.isfinite(p) & (p > 0)\n    last = float(p[np.flatnonzero(good)[-1]]) if good.any() else 1.0\n    logp = np.full(p.shape, np.nan)\n    np.log(p, out=logp, where=good)\n    r = np.diff(logp)\n    idx = pd.DatetimeIndex(context.index)\n    gaps = np.asarray((idx[1:] - idx[:-1]).total_seconds()) != 60\n    r[gaps] = np.nan\n    age = np.asarray((idx[-1] - idx[1:]).total_seconds()) / 60\n    return last, r, age\n\n\ndef ewma(r, age, half_life, absolute=False):\n    good = np.isfinite(r)\n    if not good.any():\n        return 1e-7\n    x = np.abs(r[good]) if absolute else r[good]**2\n    w = np.exp2(-age[good] / half_life)\n    if w.sum() < 1e-20:\n        w = np.ones_like(w)\n    value = float(np.sum(w*x)/np.sum(w))\n    return value*value*np.pi/2 if absolute else value\n\n\ndef simulate(asset, start_time, time_increment=300, time_length=86400,\n             num_simulations=1000, context_prices=None):\n    seed = int(os.environ.get(\"SYNTH_BENCHMARK_SEED\", \"20261108\"))\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    last, r, age = valid_returns(context_prices)\n    short = time_length <= 3600\n    variants = {\"ewma6\": (360, 30), \"ewma24\": (1440, 120), \"ewma72\": (4320, 360)}\n    if VARIANT in variants:\n        hl = variants[VARIANT][int(short)]\n        var = ewma(r, age, hl)\n    else:\n        h1, h2 = (60, 720) if short else (720, 4320)\n        v1, v2 = ewma(r, age, h1), ewma(r, age, h2)\n        var = 0.75*v1 + 0.25*v2\n        if VARIANT in (\"abs_ewma\", \"blend_abs\"):\n            absolute = .75*ewma(r, age, h1, True) + .25*ewma(r, age, h2, True)\n            var = absolute if VARIANT == \"abs_ewma\" else .5*(var + absolute)\n    sigma = np.sqrt(max(var, 1e-14) * time_increment / 60)\n    steps = time_length // time_increment + 1\n    shocks = rng.normal(0.0, sigma, size=(num_simulations, steps))\n    shocks[:, 0] = 0.0\n    paths = last*np.exp(np.cumsum(shocks, axis=1))\n    return (start_time, time_increment, *paths.tolist())\n\"\"\"Fit timestamped seasonal variance priors solely from earlier snapshot data.\n\nTraining uses locally normalized squared returns, not the evaluation metric.\nAll CRPS experiments remain in predict.py with the official backtester.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nimport argparse\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\nimport json\nimport os\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\nfrom explore import load_asset\nfrom run_suite import CRYPTO, OTHER, ROOT\n\nCLOCKS = ['UTC', 'America/New_York', 'Europe/London']\n\n\ndef fit_asset(asset, dates, lookback, bins, statistic='variance'):\n    frame = load_asset(asset)\n    close = np.asarray(frame.close, dtype=float)\n    logp = np.log(close)\n    returns = pd.Series(np.diff(logp, prepend=np.nan), index=frame.index)\n    r2 = returns.abs() if statistic == 'absolute' else returns**2\n    # Remove slow scale changes before pooling clock bins. Centering here is\n    # inside the training sample only; its final days are refitted for each cutoff.\n    fitted = {}\n    for date in dates:\n        end = pd.Timestamp(date, tz='UTC')\n        train = r2.loc[(r2.index < end) & (r2.index >= end-pd.Timedelta(days=lookback))]\n        if train.notna().sum() < 7*1440:\n            continue\n        daily = train.resample('1D').mean()\n        level = daily.rolling(15, center=True, min_periods=3).mean()\n        norm = train / level.reindex(train.index.floor('D')).to_numpy()\n        # One enormous print must not determine a clock bin for months.\n        norm = norm.clip(upper=10 if statistic == 'absolute' else 100)\n        good = np.isfinite(norm.to_numpy())\n        samples = norm.to_numpy()[good]\n        snapshot = {'training_days': int(daily.notna().sum()), 'clocks': {}}\n        for clock in CLOCKS:\n            idx = norm.index.tz_convert(clock)\n            dow = np.asarray(idx.day_of_week)\n            tod = np.asarray((idx.hour*60 + idx.minute)*bins//1440)\n            group = dow*bins+tod\n            sums = np.bincount(group[good], weights=samples, minlength=7*bins).reshape(7,bins)\n            counts = np.bincount(group[good], minlength=7*bins).reshape(7,bins)\n            mean = np.divide(sums,counts,out=np.ones_like(sums),where=counts>0)\n            if statistic == 'absolute':\n                mean = mean**2\n            snapshot['clocks'][clock] = {'raw': mean.round(7).tolist(), 'counts': counts.tolist()}\n        fitted[date] = snapshot\n    return asset, fitted\n\n\ndef main():\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n    ap.add_argument('--lookback', type=int, default=120)\n    ap.add_argument('--bins',type=int,default=96)\n    ap.add_argument('--statistic',choices=['variance','absolute'],default='variance')\n    ap.add_argument('--workers',type=int,default=4)\n    ap.add_argument('--output',type=Path,default=Path('agent/artifacts/seasonality.json'))\n    args = ap.parse_args()\n    os.chdir(ROOT)\n    dates = list(pd.date_range('2025-09-01','2026-11-01',freq='MS').strftime('%Y-%m-%d'))+['2026-11-09']\n    with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=args.workers) as pool:\n        results = list(pool.map(lambda a: fit_asset(a,dates,args.lookback,args.bins,args.statistic), CRYPTO+OTHER))\n    payload={'bins':args.bins, 'lookback_days':args.lookback,'statistic':args.statistic,'cutoff':'2026-11-08','assets':dict(results)}\n    args.output.parent.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)\n    args.output.write_text(json.dumps(payload,separators=(',',':'))+'\\n')\n    print('Saved', args.output, 'bytes', args.output.stat().st_size)\n    for a, fits in results:\n        print(a, len(fits), 'as-of snapshots')\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
