{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_379","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '210,390p' agent/CAMPAIGN.md\"","aggregated_output":"- **Miner dashboard analytics API** (`https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1`, schema at\n  `https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1/openapi.json`): the public backend behind the miner\n  dashboard — scoreboards, leaderboards, reward pools. Read the OpenAPI schema to discover the\n  endpoints. Same rule as the scores API: studying the field is encouraged, copying an\n  individual miner's served paths is not.\n- **Pretrained models & Hugging Face**: allowed and in scope. The sandbox has network access to\n  the HF Hub and PyPI; the GPU is yours and sits idle unless you use it. Time-series foundation\n  models, fine-tuning, or classical ML are all legitimate strategies — as is deciding, with\n  evidence, that they don't improve on closed-form calibration. If you go this way: download weights\n  EARLY (bandwidth is part of your clock), set `HF_HOME` inside your workspace, and remember\n  your champion must run OFFLINE — weights must be committed to your git workspace, total under\n  a few GB (the harness collects your repo as a git bundle).\n- **Serving latency**: the live validator gives you roughly the prompt's lead time (~1 minute)\n  to respond — that is the hard bound. But the evaluation of your champion generates several\n  thousand prompts, and your own backtests will too: at more than a few seconds per prompt\n  (1000 paths, this hardware), your iteration loop and your evaluation both become the\n  bottleneck. Measure your per-prompt latency early and treat ~5 s as the practical ceiling.\n- **The web**: open for knowledge — searching the literature (arXiv, papers, reference\n  implementations) is encouraged — but NEVER for fetching prices or market data (see hard\n  rules).\n\n## Your envelope\n\n- Credit budget: 100.0 USD. Deadline: 18 h of wall-clock time.\n- The `BUDGET.md` file in your workspace is maintained by the harness: READ IT AT THE START OF\n  EACH ITERATION. When it contains a LANDING ORDER, it is non-negotiable.\n- Hardware: GCP n1-standard-16: 16 vCPU / 60GB RAM / 1x NVIDIA T4 (16GB VRAM; Turing, no bf16). Your sandbox gets 14 CPU, 48GB RAM and the GPU. torch is already in your pyproject — no need to add it. ONLY ONE GPU job at a time; cap the memory of your heavy jobs.\n- **Parallel work shares this envelope.** Every agent or job you start — subagents, workflows,\n  background runs — spends from the same credit budget and runs on the same machine. The budget in\n  `BUDGET.md` and the hardware above are totals, whatever a tool's own guidance says about token\n  cost.\n- **You are expected to use your envelope.** Iterating is the job: measure, change one thing,\n  measure again. **Nominating is final** — the first time you stop while `agent/CHAMPION`\n  exists, your campaign is over: you will not be resumed, and every remaining dollar and hour\n  is forfeited. Until then you may keep iterating and amend your nomination. Nominate early\n  only when your journal contains EVIDENCE that further iteration would not improve your Score.\n  **A champion is MANDATORY.** What is scored is how strong your champion is, not whether you dared\n  to nominate one: ending with no `agent/CHAMPION` is a Score of 0 and a wasted leg — strictly worse\n  than nominating something imperfect.\n\n## Constraints (they are what make the result valid)\n\n1. **No-lookahead**: a prediction at `start_time` uses only information available at\n   `start_time`. Your champion is evaluated on the 10 days FOLLOWING your data cutoff: treat\n   everything after **2026-11-08** as nonexistent. Querying ANY API (scores, rewards,\n   dashboard) for times after 2026-11-08 is lookahead into your own evaluation window and\n   invalidates the result. Audited on your transcript.\n2. **Prices = snapshot only.** No fetching market data online (price APIs, exchanges,\n   aggregators). The web is for knowledge only. Audited on your transcript.\n   Event dates come only from that calendar: a table typed from memory or fetched from the web goes\n   stale and wrong where your miner serves.\n3. **Scoring = synth-lib.** Every CRPS evaluation goes through `synth_lib.backtester`\n   (via `agent/predict.py`). One implementation of the metric is what makes results comparable.\n4. Not allowed: falsifying the journal or results; reproducing another participant's served\n   output as your own.\n5. **Keep binary and encoded data out of your terminal output.** Read parquet with pandas and\n   `.npy` with numpy — never `cat` a parquet, a git bundle, or model weights, and never pipe\n   base64 into your context. A screenful of encoded bytes buys you nothing, costs context, and\n   is a known trigger for content classifiers that will end your turn.\n6. **Start from public sources only**: synth-lib, synth-subnet, the Synth API, and your own\n   ideas. The operator's existing miner models are not part of your materials and must not\n   inform your work. Nothing of that kind belongs in your environment; if any of it turns up\n   there, leave it unread and note it in `suggestions.md`.\n7. Your final champion must expose, in `agent/modeling.py`:\n   `simulate(asset, start_time, time_increment, time_length, num_simulations, context_prices)`\n   -> tuple `(start_timestamp, time_increment, *paths)` — each path: a list of\n   `time_length//time_increment + 1` floats starting at the last price in the context.\n   **The `context_prices` contract, exactly**: a minute-indexed pd.DataFrame over the\n   `CONTEXT_DAYS` days preceding `start_time`, with columns `open, high, low, close, volume,\n   trade_count`. `close` is the series you are scored against; the rest are context. It MAY\n   CONTAIN NaN in every column, on whole days before an asset's first trade or before the\n   snapshot's first day too — your `simulate()` must tolerate them (drop, never interpolate).\n   Evaluation passes the raw frame: handle gaps inside your model, not in a loader you hope\n   evaluation shares.\n   **`CONTEXT_DAYS` is yours to choose**, and to measure like any other parameter: a plain\n   integer at module level in `agent/modeling.py`, from 3 to 30, not computed (a module without\n   it gets 7). `agent/predict.py`, the interface check, the scoring and the live miner all read\n   it, and any other value fails every one of them. One value serves every competition: slice a\n   shorter context inside `simulate()` where you want one. The interface check reports the wall\n   time of one call at your length.\n   Runnable OFFLINE (weights/artifacts committed or included in the workspace).\n8. **If your model uses randomness at all, seed it from `SYNTH_BENCHMARK_SEED`.** Nothing\n   here asks you to introduce randomness — a deterministic champion ignores this rule. If you\n   do use it, read the variable once at the top of `simulate()` and seed every generator your\n   model touches from it: Python's `random`, NumPy, torch, and anything carrying its own. It\n   is absent during your own runs and set only at evaluation, so fall back to a seed of your\n   choosing when it is missing. What you may never do is hardcode a seed that ignores the\n   variable, or leave a generator unseeded so the clock or OS entropy supplies it.\n\n## Required deliverables (in `agent/`)\n\nWrite `journal.md`, `report.md`, and `suggestions.md` in English — these reports are published\nto the Synth miner community.\n\n- `journal.md`: one entry per iteration — Hypothesis / Change / Result (numbers) / Verdict\n  (keep-discard) / Lesson. As you go: this is your memory if you crash.\n- `report.md`: your final report, explainer style (audience = Synth miners). Sections: context,\n  approach, experiments (failures included), results, limitations, what you'd do with more\n  budget.\n- `CHAMPION`: YAML — `sha` (an EXISTING git commit in your workspace, not a placeholder),\n  `agent_dir: agent`, `profiles`, `notes`. `profiles` must list the formats your champion\n  supports: `[low, high]` if you cover both 24h AND 1h (expected), `[low]` if you could only\n  handle the 24h. `notes` MUST include your champion's backtested **CRPS per configuration**,\n  produced by the official backtester (`agent/predict.py`) at 1000 paths, alongside the CRPS of\n  whatever you started from — a nomination without measured numbers is incomplete. The harness checks the interface as soon as\n  this file appears (result in BUDGET.md) — fix it if the check fails.\n- `suggestions.md`: anything that falls outside your scope.\n- you must commit all deliverables to your workspace git repo before the deadline.\n\n## Method (your choice, but recommended)\n\nYou choose your own research questions, hypotheses, splits, and criteria, and your own in-sample\nand out-of-sample windows: there is no holdout rule, and the field bundle covers the span DATA.md\ngives. What works here:\nwrite the hypothesis BEFORE the experiment, pre-registered kill thresholds, validation on 2\nwindows of opposite regimes (within the limits of the snapshot), one variable at a time,\nfrequent commits. Watch out for the edges of eval windows: only backtest windows fully covered\nby the snapshot. Lessons earlier agents paid for:\n\n- **Explain your CRPS before you tune it.** Break a backtest's CRPS down by asset, configuration,\n  hour of day and day, and read the worst prompts: the realized path, your fan, and the field's CRPS\n  on the same prompt (`backtest()` returns every miner's CRPS per prompt in `prompt_df`; save it from\n  `agent/predict.py`). For each anomaly, find the explanation: a gap in the context, an event, a\n  regime change, a bug in your model. Write it in your journal before you change anything. An\n  explained anomaly tells you what to fix; a sweep over an unexplained one tunes to noise.\n- **Price a residual before fitting it.** For a Gaussian fan, expected CRPS at scale σ against\n  the right σ* is about 1 + ¼·(ln σ/σ*)² times the optimum: a ±20 % global scale error costs about\n  1 %, and a width correction across buckets can gain at most about ¼ of the variance of the\n  log width ratios. A minute of arithmetic tells you whether a day of sweeping can pay.\n- **Test a feature by what it adds to a fast EWMA of realized volatility**, not by its own\n  predictive power. Range estimators, volume, trade counts and the leverage effect act on future\n  volatility through realized volatility, which the EWMA has already priced. What it cannot hold\n  is information outside the context: the calendar, and anything older than the `CONTEXT_DAYS`\n  `simulate()` receives.\n- **Validate an event effect leave-one-event-out.** A type occurs 8 to 12 times a year, so a\n  multiplier fitted and tested on the same releases is in-sample.\n- **Check what `simulate()` can see before building on it**: one asset's `CONTEXT_DAYS` context\n  per call, nothing else. A feature that needs another asset's current prices cannot be served.\n- **Count the asset-window cells where a change helps**, across disjoint windows, not only its\n  average: a small gain signed the same way in most cells survives; a larger one that flips sign\n  between windows is noise.\n- **Do not tune on the last weeks before your cutoff alone.** They hold one regime, and your\n  champion is scored on the weeks after the cutoff, which may hold another. Validate a change on\n  windows from different regimes across the whole span, and keep it only if it holds on them.\n\n## Resuming after a crash\n\nIf you restart with no context: reread this file, then `BUDGET.md`, then your `journal.md`,\nand continue where you left off.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_380","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat DATA.md'","aggregated_output":"# DATA.md — the shape of your market data (generated from THIS snapshot at setup)\n\nEach day is one parquet file, `date=YYYY-MM-DD.parquet`, a full 1440-row minute grid\nwith columns `open, high, low, close, volume, trade_count`. `close` is the scored series.\n`close` is the venue's 1-minute candle close (the last trade of that minute); the other\ncolumns describe the same minute. For the Hyperliquid-routed assets each minute is\naggregated from the venue's 1-second candles (first open, highest high, lowest low, last\nclose), the same values as the venue's 1-minute candle.\n`source`/`ingested_at`/`is_final` are ingestion provenance — ignore them. Semantics:\n\n- **`trade_count` 0** = no trade that minute: the venue prints a flat candle (open, high, low\n  and close at the previous close, volume 0), and the validator scores against it. NOT market\n  hours: these venues trade 24/7. Trade density varies by asset and era — see the table.\n- **NaN close** = no price at all: before the market's first trade, or a hole in the feed.\n- **Missing file** = before that market's first trade, or before the snapshot's first day. A\n  context reaching back before an asset's first file starts with NaN days.\n- Scoring drops NaN per prompt. Never interpolate: inventing prices in the gaps is how\n  you fool your own validation.\n\nPer-asset coverage, measured from this snapshot (untraded% = share of minutes without a\ntrade, flat or NaN, among the asset's real days; first/last 30d columns show density drift —\nearly months of newer listings are genuinely thin, low realized volatility there is real):\n\n| asset | first day | last day | real days | untraded% total | untraded% first 30d | untraded% last 30d |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| AAPLX | 2025-11-21 | 2026-11-08 | 353 | 39.1% | 60.1% | 19.0% |\n| BTC | 2025-08-01 | 2026-11-08 | 465 | 0.0% | 0.0% | 0.0% |\n| ETH | 2025-08-01 | 2026-11-08 | 465 | 0.0% | 0.0% | 0.0% |\n| GOOGLX | 2025-11-18 | 2026-11-08 | 356 | 21.5% | 23.9% | 1.9% |\n| HYPE | 2025-08-01 | 2026-11-08 | 465 | 0.1% | 0.1% | 0.0% |\n| NVDAX | 2025-11-12 | 2026-11-08 | 362 | 9.7% | 14.9% | 3.2% |\n| SOL | 2025-08-01 | 2026-11-08 | 465 | 0.0% | 0.0% | 0.0% |\n| SP500 | 2026-03-18 | 2026-11-08 | 236 | 0.9% | 2.8% | 0.2% |\n| SPCX | 2026-05-17 | 2026-11-08 | 176 | 3.0% | 4.6% | 3.3% |\n| TSLAX | 2025-11-13 | 2026-11-08 | 361 | 14.3% | 26.2% | 9.4% |\n| WTIOIL | 2026-01-06 | 2026-11-08 | 307 | 10.4% | 58.8% | 1.4% |\n| XAU | 2025-12-22 | 2026-11-08 | 322 | 9.7% | 39.2% | 1.4% |\n| XRP | 2025-08-01 | 2026-11-08 | 465 | 0.0% | 0.0% | 0.0% |\n\n`timestamp` loads as `datetime64[us, UTC]`. An integer view of it (`.view(\"int64\")`) counts in that unit:\nderive minute indices from the dtype, never by assuming nanoseconds.\n\n**Offline field-scores bundle** (`SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT`): scored\nprompts covered **2026-07-24 → 2026-11-07**. Rank backtests outside this\nrange have no field to compare against — do not query the live API for more.\n\nPer competition: `backtest()` drops each window's first `window_days` of reward rounds (see\nCAMPAIGN.md), so the first round the bundle can rank is its first scored day plus `window_days`.\nThe cadence is the spacing of the prompts the validator SCORED, which is sparser than the\nprompts it issues:\n\n| competition | scored prompts | window_days | first ranked round | scored prompts per asset |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| com-equ-24h | 2026-07-24 → 2026-11-07 | 10 | 2026-08-03 | about 24 a day (every 60 min), 2026-07-24 → 2026-10-29; about 10 a day (every 144 min), 2026-10-30 → 2026-11-07 |\n| crypto-24h | 2026-07-24 → 2026-11-07 | 10 | 2026-08-03 | about 24 a day (every 60 min) |\n| crypto-1h | 2026-07-24 → 2026-11-07 | 5 | 2026-07-29 | about 144 a day (every 10 min) |\n\ncom-equ-24h's prompts scored since 2026-10-30 start at the same times every day (UTC):\n\n| asset | start times |\n|---|---|\n| XAU | 01:45 04:09 06:33 08:57 11:21 13:45 16:09 18:33 20:57 23:21 |\n| NVDAX | 02:21 04:45 07:09 09:33 11:57 14:21 16:45 19:09 21:33 23:57 |\n| GOOGLX | 00:15 02:39 05:03 07:27 09:51 12:15 14:39 17:03 19:27 21:51 |\n| TSLAX | 00:33 02:57 05:21 07:45 10:09 12:33 14:57 17:21 19:45 22:09 |\n| AAPLX | 00:51 03:15 05:39 08:03 10:27 12:51 15:15 17:39 20:03 22:27 |\n| WTIOIL | 01:09 03:33 05:57 08:21 10:45 13:09 15:33 17:57 20:21 22:45 |\n| SPCX | 01:27 03:51 06:15 08:39 11:03 13:27 15:51 18:15 20:39 23:03 |\n| SP500 | 02:03 04:27 06:51 09:15 11:39 14:03 16:27 18:51 21:15 23:39 |\n\n**Cached realized paths** (13 assets): **2026-06-20 → 2026-11-07**. Where a\nprompt's slice of the price store contains NaN, scoring substitutes the validator's own\narray for that prompt. Outside this range it cannot, and the prompt is scored on the\npoints that remain — which is a smaller CRPS from a thinner reference, not a better model.\n\nSteps with no price change in those paths, the arrays CRPS is computed against: XAU (5 min) 11.6%, SOL (1 min) 9.4%, XRP (1 min) 7.7%, SPCX (5 min) 6.3%, AAPLX (5 min) 5.6%, BTC (1 min) 5.3%, NVDAX (5 min) 5.2%, SP500 (5 min) 5.0%, GOOGLX (5 min) 3.9%, SOL (5 min) 3.9%, TSLAX (5 min) 3.2%, XRP (5 min) 3.0%, ETH (1 min) 2.6%, HYPE (1 min) 1.3%, WTIOIL (5 min) 1.2%, BTC (5 min) 0.6%, ETH (5 min) 0.4%, HYPE (5 min) 0.3%.\n\n**Daylight saving.** Prices, prompts and the calendar are in UTC. The US session and the US releases\nkeep New York time, and European hours keep London time, so in UTC they move an hour at each switch.\nSwitches between 2025-08-01 and 2026-11-08, on the day the clock changes, with the offset after it:\n\n| clock | switches in the snapshot | offset on 2026-11-08 | next switch |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| New York | 2025-11-02 → UTC-5, 2026-03-08 → UTC-4, 2026-11-01 → UTC-5 | UTC-5 | 2027-03-14 → UTC-4 |\n| London | 2025-10-26 → UTC+0, 2026-03-29 → UTC+1, 2026-10-25 → UTC+0 | UTC+0 | 2027-03-28 → UTC+1 |\n\nThe NYSE session, 09:30–16:00 in New York, is 13:30–20:00 UTC in summer time\n(UTC-4) and 14:30–21:00 UTC in winter time (UTC-5).\n\n**Scheduled events** (`calendar/events.parquet`; your champion reads it at `$SYNTH_BENCHMARK_CALENDAR`):\n226 events, 2025-08-01 → 2027-01-18: every date\npublished by the cutoff, upcoming ones included. Columns: `time_utc`, `event`, `scope` (`all`, or the\nassets it concerns, comma-separated), `detail`, `source`. Times are scheduled New York times in UTC,\nso they move an hour with daylight saving: US macro data at 08:30, FOMC statements at 14:00, the EIA\npetroleum report Wednesdays at 10:30 (holiday weeks differ). Earnings are anchored at the session,\n08:00 before the open and 16:00 after the close; an NYSE closure at the 09:30 open that does not\nhappen, an early close at 13:00. A date published or moved after the cutoff is not here.\n\nEvents of each type before and after the snapshot's last day, and the other types released at the\nsame minute, whose effects cannot be told apart from it there:\n\n| event | before cutoff | after cutoff | at the same time as |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CPI | 14 | 2 | — |\n| EARNINGS | 22 | 1 | — |\n| EIA_PETROLEUM | 66 | 8 | — |\n| FOMC | 10 | 1 | — |\n| GDP | 14 | 2 | PCE 14/16 |\n| NFP | 15 | 1 | RETAIL_SALES 2/16 |\n| NYSE_CLOSED | 11 | 4 | — |\n| NYSE_EARLY_CLOSE | 2 | 2 | — |\n| PCE | 15 | 2 | GDP 14/17 |\n| PPI | 14 | 2 | RETAIL_SALES 3/16 |\n| RETAIL_SALES | 16 | 2 | NFP 2/18, PPI 3/18 |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
