{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_412","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'dependen|correla|microstruct|Hurst|rough|variance ratio|AR\\\\(|MA\\\\(' agent/journal.md agent/report.md agent/research_plan.md && tail -4 agent/results/calendar_complete_wide_field.log && tail -4 agent/results/candidate_general.log\"","aggregated_output":"agent/research_plan.md:17:Initial development scored windows: 2026-08-17 through 2026-08-23 and 2026-10-05 through 2026-10-11. Recent confirmation: 2026-11-01 through 2026-11-07. Older tests will use CRPS-only with the latest 1h formula and fully covered prices. The regime characterization may lead to additional windows; record choices before scoring.\nagent/report.md:29:Robust five-minute seasonal profiles improve on the original fifteen-minute profile: development changes are -0.017% / -0.349% / -0.081%, and confirmation changes -0.158% / -0.252% / -0.139%. The November gold regression remains visible rather than being removed from the average. Refitting the regression under the new profile does not add a reliable improvement, so the original feature definitions and weights remain the reference. The forecast shape can be calibrated independently of those features.\nagent/report.md:37:**Known events.** Forward release kernels are fitted from earlier events relative to causal volatility forecasts. Coincident GDP/PCE releases are treated together, and correlated assets at one release do not count as independent events. Earnings have broad kernels because their timestamps are session anchors. In a 180-day event-centered test, forward calendar adjustments reduce one-hour crypto CRPS from 1622.017546 to 1581.481152 (-2.50%) and other24 from 3167.322481 to 3145.300386 (-0.70%). On ordinary sparse grids the effects are tiny and mixed. Removing event shocks from historical EWMA does not help; it is discarded.\nagent/report.md:68:The windows are research samples and have been reused for model selection. Asset-window sign counts are stability diagnostics, not independent statistical trials. Adjacent forecast horizons overlap, and assets share market shocks. Event effects have especially few independent dates; a calendar can predict timing but cannot predict the sign or magnitude of an unscheduled surprise.\nagent/report.md:76:This section will be finalized after the ongoing studies. Priority questions are calibration under live data gaps, stability across changing listing liquidity, and uncertainty about the average future volatility rather than more unconstrained point-forecast features. More independent event dates would be more informative than a larger grid of multipliers fitted to the same releases.\nagent/journal.md:5:**Hypothesis.** The seven-day constant Gaussian starter is miscalibrated during changes in volatility and across equity/commodity trading sessions. The 1h score also rewards a calibrated distribution of future realized volatility, which an independent Gaussian fan may miss.\nagent/journal.md:13:**Lesson.** Forecasts must model increment distributions throughout the horizon, not just terminal prices. Local clocks, gaps and finite-ensemble uncertainty are substantive calibration issues.\nagent/journal.md:17:Both development windows, 7 scored days each, every 120 minutes at minute 17, 1000 paths: baseline CRYPTO_24H **4802.283427** (840 prompts), COM_EQU_24H **2004.899638** (1344), CRYPTO_1H **1027.941065** (840). Realized coverage is 100%. Generation including load/write is roughly 0.039 s per 24h prompt and 0.011 s per 1h prompt. Results retained under `results/baseline_dev`.\nagent/journal.md:47:**Verdict.** Keep robust scale estimation as a candidate, with confirmation required. Do not select the fastest 24h estimator: it worsens August crypto by 0.18% despite helping October. Raw mixed EWMA gains only 0.74% in crypto 24h, below the predeclared 1% threshold; its role is a fixed reference for the independent seasonality experiments. The absolute-return estimator's lower scale is useful, but will be rechecked after changing the innovation distribution to avoid double-counting tail concentration.\nagent/journal.md:53:**Hypothesis.** Predictable variation over the trading week explains a large part of the commodity/equity CRPS deficit. New York time should transfer through DST better than UTC for the equities; London may fit gold better. The effect on crypto should be smaller and asset-dependent.\nagent/journal.md:57:**Result.** Descriptive measurements: summer weekday New York peak/trough minute-sigma ratios are BTC 2.26, ETH 2.06, SOL 1.93, XRP 1.90, HYPE 1.67; XAU 4.07, NVDAX 5.31, GOOGLX 5.41, TSLAX 7.34, AAPLX 5.62, WTIOIL 2.71, SPCX 4.51, SP500 3.54. Most equities peak in the 09:00 New York hour. Full-history five-minute variance divided by five times minute variance is 0.89 for AAPLX and 0.93 for NVDAX, about 0.98–1.02 for most others. These are descriptions, not CRPS results. Paired scoring pending.\nagent/journal.md:69:Independent 7-day scored windows ending 2026-07-20 and 2026-11-08, identical 120-minute offset grids and 1000 paths: baseline CRPS is **3831.087636 / 3114.391572 / 814.313609**, New York seasonal EWMA **3730.014936 / 2912.866817 / 776.619142** (crypto24 / other24 / crypto1). Every one of the 36 asset-window cells improves. Seasonality adds to the EWMA itself on 16/16 other24 development cells and 10/10 crypto1 cells.\nagent/journal.md:77:**Hypothesis.** A forecast of future variance is not a known constant. Independent Student innovations or a mixture over future volatility may fit the concentration and tails of returns better, and a volatility process should improve the 1h block-volatility CRPS.\nagent/journal.md:79:**Change.** Hold the New York seasonal EWMA variance forecast fixed. Compare variance-standardized Student t innovations (df 3, 5, 8), a path-wide lognormal volatility multiplier (log standard deviation .2, .4, .6), and a stationary log-volatility AR(1) (.4, .6, half-life 30 minutes for 1h or 180 minutes for 24h). Normalize each multiplier to preserve its expected squared scale. Seed all NumPy draws from the benchmark seed. No change to the estimator or fitting data.\nagent/journal.md:89:**Hypothesis.** Multiple past volatility timescales may support a better forecast of average future scale. Range, volume, trade counts and signed returns must demonstrate incremental benefit over those volatility features, not merely correlate with future volatility on their own.\nagent/journal.md:107:**Verdict.** Require positive forward-only event results across multiple dates, plus no material regression on the four general windows. Report conditional event CRPS separately from all-prompt CRPS. Do not count correlated assets at one release as independent events.\nagent/journal.md:128:**Result.** Pending. Descriptive return variance scaling in June–November is close to diffusive for most assets: five-minute variance ratios are about .98–1.07 of five times minute variance. Longer crypto intervals show mild mean reversion, but the likely aggregate gain from that smaller effect is less than the remaining seasonality/calibration questions.\nagent/journal.md:130:**Verdict.** Require improvements on both development windows, most asset-window cells, and independent confirmation. Prefer the lower-resolution or more strongly pooled model when differences are within sampling noise.\nagent/journal.md:164:**Result.** Pending. The theoretical order of finite-ensemble CRPS bias is 1/N, so a gain much larger than about a tenth of a percent from sampling alone would need explanation. A smaller consistent improvement is acceptable here after independent-seed replication, unlike an unconfirmed extra forecasting feature.\nagent/journal.md:201:A mean-scale-preserving lognormal mixture with log standard deviation .4 and stratified normal coordinates scores **4688.7811 / 1767.6311 / 980.0071**. Student-t(5) scores **4688.5521 / 1770.7012 / 980.5720**. The changes are small enough to require confirmation and independent seeds. Conditional volatility quantiles plus within-path volatility score **4684.5253 / 1770.2478 / 979.9498**; preserving the point model's average instead gives **4689.3396 / 1768.2599 / 979.9828**. Both proceed to confirmation, without claiming that a fitted uncertainty model has yet beaten a simpler distribution.\nagent/journal.md:209:## Iteration 13 — learned volatility through the forecast horizon\nagent/journal.md:233:### Iterations 6–8: first independent confirmation\nagent/journal.md:317:The latest fitted minute-00 amplitudes are only about 1.04–1.10 times the containing five-minute mean; the later minutes are typically 0.93–1.00. These are far smaller deviations than the weekday/session effects. The rough log-width-squared CRPS bound predicts a gain of only hundredths of a percent once the minute-return component's weight is considered. Score the two predeclared patterns, but do not pursue an extensive sub-minute-clock sweep.\nagent/journal.md:321:**Hypothesis.** The learned point model predicts a log-average future scale. Under uncertainty, its geometric mean need not equal the width appropriate to either a Gaussian fan or a mean-preserving scale mixture. A residual log-scale standard deviation near .3 corresponds to roughly a 2% Gaussian-width or 5% mean-mixture-width correction, not a reason to fit another complex model.\nagent/journal.md:347:**Verdict.** Verify gains on all four windows, at actual field timestamps and under an independent seed before promotion. If components overlap or regress, retain the simpler subset. Every serving option will be fixed explicitly in modeling.py at landing, not selected by a research environment variable.\nagent/journal.md:397:### Independent-seed qualification\nNVDAX  86400 2026-11-08 CRPS=1966.34115 n=2053 s/prompt=0.063\nGOOGLX 86400 2026-11-08 CRPS=1718.74241 n=2052 s/prompt=0.064\nTSLAX  86400 2026-11-08 CRPS=2350.97054 n=2052 s/prompt=0.065\nAAPLX  86400 2026-11-08 CRPS=1415.21283 n=2053 s/prompt=0.065\nRunning candidate_shape; budget checked.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
